{"id":"h269","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"What is the force between two positive charges of 100\\mu coulombs and 200\\mu coulombs, respectively, when they are separated a distance of 3 centimeters?\nIs the correct answer \"2 x 10^5nt\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"Coulomb's law F = k q1 q2 / r^2 with k ≈ 8.99×10^9 applies to this problem.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The charges convert to 1.00×10^-4 C and 2.00×10^-4 C, and the distance to 0.03 m.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The calculation gives approximately 2.0×10^5 N.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Both charges are positive, so the force is repulsive.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The enormous magnitude might suggest an error.","confidence":0.15,"lean":-1,"theta":0.2356,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"100 μC is a large charge and the calculation is internally consistent, so the large magnitude is not a real objection.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"'nt' means newtons, the standard SI unit of force.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The result rounds to 2×10^5 N, so the stated answer is correct.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Physical plausibility is only a check, not a counterargument, so the calculation decides the matter.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":7,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":4,"J":-0.6,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":2,"j":5,"J":0.6,"relation":"sup